# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Lanternina — .gitignore
#
# This repository will be public. Two categories below are not "untidy files",
# they are things that must never enter a git history nobody can recall:
#   (1) key material — the sealing keys are what make the safety and approval
#       guarantees real; a leaked key lets anyone forge approved content;
#   (2) anything about the learner — scans of her work, her profile, her
#       routines, the approval ledger. None of it is ever committed, not even
#       as a test fixture or a screenshot.
# When in doubt, add it here first and ask later.
# ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────

# ── Key material ─────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
secrets/
*.key
*.pem
*.pfx
*.p12
*.crt
*.cer
*.der
*.jks
*.keystore
*.kdbx
*.ppk
id_rsa*
id_ed25519*
known_hosts*
authorized_keys
.env
.env.*
!.env.example
# ESP32 Wi-Fi + broker credentials, generated from the template
wifi_secrets.h

# Local overrides of any kind. The pattern is deliberately broad.
*.local.yaml
*.local.yml
*.local.json
*.local.toml
secrets.local.*

# Azure CLI / MSAL token caches, if a tool ever drops one in the tree
.azure/
.msal*

# ── Learner data — never committed, in any form ──────────────────────────────
# A place to keep something that has to stay on this machine and has no rule of its
# own yet. Putting a file here is the answer to "where do I park this for now".
private/
# Where a house with no person in it writes: the screens it drew, the sheets it composed,
# and the transcript of an afternoon. The transcript is the reason this line exists —
# a record of what somebody did is exactly what must not enter a public history, and it is
# only written at all because the somebody is whoever ran `python -m tools.pretend`.
pretend/
# The experiments run against the simulated house. Everything a run makes is invented —
# there is no person in it — so what keeps the pixels out is size and not privacy: a page is
# roughly a megabyte and a soak is dozens of them. The notes are kept, because what was
# measured and what somebody thought of it are the parts worth having later.
#
# `experiments/**` and not `experiments/*`: the second only matches direct children, so the
# first commit of this took a transcript and two run files in with it.
experiments/**
!experiments/**/
!experiments/**/notes.md
!experiments/README.md
# And the one small file that says what a reader made of a batch: the findings, the question
# and the answer worked out per afternoon, and the fingerprint of the prompt that wrote
# them. A few kilobytes of invented material, and keeping it out cost the one thing this
# apparatus is for — on 3 September 2026 the only surviving record of five judged batches was
# a table somebody had retyped into `ideas/`. A drift trail that does not survive a change of
# session is not one.
#
# `runs.json` stays out: it carries every afternoon document in full, which is 560 kB against
# 100 kB, and those documents are already the `NN.json` beside it.
!experiments/**/judged.json
# Browser tooling drops screenshots, accessibility snapshots and console dumps here;
# any of the three can carry a page of the panel with real content in it.
.playwright-mcp/
data/
captures/
pages/
scans/
sessions/
ledger/
approved/
profiles/
# Working photographs of the bench, the hardware and the printed sheets. Not
# deliverables, and too easy for a real page or a face to end up in one.
pictures/
# Any image that might be a real page rather than a synthetic fixture. Scanners
# emit TIFF, phones emit HEIC, the renderer emits SVG — all of them can carry a
# page of her handwriting, so the list is by intent, not by convenience.
*.png
*.jpg
*.jpeg
*.jpe
*.gif
*.bmp
*.tif
*.tiff
*.webp
*.heic
*.heif
*.dng
*.raw
*.ppm
*.pgm
*.pnm
*.svg
*.pdf
*.ps
!docs/diagrams/*.png
!docs/diagrams/*.svg
!tests/fixtures/**/*.png

# Anything recorded in the house. The pitch video and its raw footage are
# working material and large; neither belongs in git.
*.mp4
*.mov
*.avi
*.mkv
*.webm
*.wav
*.mp3
*.m4a
*.ogg
*.flac

# Local stores the running system writes: the approval ledger and session
# records land here if a backend is ever pointed at a file.
*.db
*.sqlite
*.sqlite3
*.csv
*.parquet

# Backups pulled off the device, and archives generally — a CM5 backup is a
# couple of gigabytes and contains everything above.
*.zip
*.tar
*.tar.gz
*.tgz
*.gz
*.7z
*.rar
*.img

# ── Model weights ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
# Nothing infers on the device, so these should never appear. Ignored anyway, so an
# experiment never turns into an accidental multi-gigabyte commit.
models/
*.gguf
*.onnx
*.safetensors

# ── Python ───────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
__pycache__/
*.py[cod]
*.egg-info/
.eggs/
build/
dist/
.venv/
venv/
.pytest_cache/
.mypy_cache/
.ruff_cache/
.coverage
htmlcov/

# ── Firmware build output ────────────────────────────────────────────────────
firmware/**/build/
*.uf2
*.elf
*.bin
*.hex

# ── Editor / OS ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
.vscode/*
!.vscode/extensions.json
.idea/
.DS_Store
Thumbs.db
*.swp

# ── Scratch ──────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────────
tmp/
*.tmp
*.bak
*.orig
*.rej
*.log
_scratch*
notes/
.ipynb_checkpoints/

# ── Reference code from other projects ───────────────────────────────────────
# Read-only material kept in the workspace to study while porting. Never part of
# this repository: it carries another project's licence, history and config.
_reference/

# ── Internal / employer material ─────────────────────────────────────────────
# Hackathon rules, policies and anything else pasted from an internal source.
# This repository is public; internal documents do not belong in it.
microsoftinfo/
hack_plan/
# Session handovers are working memory: they carry addresses, host names and
# half-finished judgements written for the next session, not for a reader.
HANDOVER*.md
**/HANDOVER*.md

# Office documents. Decks, pitch drafts and anything exported from an internal
# template arrive with metadata and comments nobody re-reads before committing.
*.docx
*.doc
*.pptx
*.ppt
*.xlsx
*.xls
